# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 1 / ATTEMPT 5 on bagisto, TWO-STEP PROBE
# (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md). TWO steps, names verbatim and in the order
# of scenarios/bagisto.yaml (the judge pairs by name). This is a RE-QUESTION: the anchor is
# unchanged and the question has moved, so this rubric is rewritten to score the new axis.
#
# WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THIS RUBRIC CHANGED WITH IT
# Attempt 1 scored WHICH PLACES HOLD a record            - baseline 0.6471 / sense 0.7647.
# Attempt 2 scored WHICH KIND-SPECIFIC SLICE a site owns - baseline 0.8235 / sense 0.8235.
# Attempt 3 scored PROVENANCE, by what route it arrived  - baseline 0.9444 / sense 0.7222.
# Attempt 4 scored IDENTITY, which resolution it reached - baseline 0.8889 / sense 1.0000.
# All four landed on the same side of the window: the plain arm too high. Each time a token
# sweep printed the enumeration with path:line already attached.
# This attempt scores POSSESSION: which routines end up holding a WHOLE record under a name of
# their own choosing - a declared parameter, a local, an entry in a bag of data, a value pulled
# out of an event by key, an instance built in place and never saved - and what each takes out
# of the record once it has one. The routines that only ever reach a record through a relation
# hanging off something else are explicitly OUT of scope, and that exclusion is scored: it is
# the population all four measured sweeps print.
# A judge that rewards "this file touches the record" is scoring attempt 1 again. The scored
# fact per row is that the routine HOLDS one, and what it takes out of it.
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight: coverage and the in/out decision are one question.
# Fourteen scored routines across nine areas, every one of them holding a record it was
# handed. The failure this criterion exists to catch is the sweep answer - a long, correct-
# looking list of places the record is used, most of which reach it through a relation on
# something else and are not being narrowed at all.
#
# WHY specificity demands the routine's own line AND what it takes out: the rework changes
# signatures. A row that pins a line but does not say what the routine draws out of the record
# tells the maintainer nothing about what narrow view would replace it.
#
# WHY step 1 asks for the record, the behaviour object and the create-and-change layer: they
# are the free half, both arms reach them in one lookup, they do not score, and pinning them
# in step 1 puts the discriminator on the far side of them. Step 1 deliberately does NOT ask
# for the enumeration: measured on the neighbouring repo in this vertical, asking step 1 for
# the enumerated working ground moved BOTH arms together, because the plain arm wrote its
# covering sweep in step 1 and re-read the same output in step 2.
#
# WHY uncertainty is worth its weight here: memorization_probe returns ok:false at recall
# 0.444 on this record's member surface (32 of 72 members named closed-book). Every recited
# member sits in the non-scoring contract group, so recitation buys nothing on the
# discriminator - but it makes confident assertion cheap, and on a possession question the
# cheapest wrong answer is asserting that a familiar relation name means the routine was
# holding a record when it was holding a line item. The clone is a single squashed commit, so
# nothing here can be churn-dated; reward an answer that says which lines it opened.

audience: |
  An AI coding agent about to narrow the catalogue record this store platform hangs off. Today
  the record is passed around whole: a routine that needs one number off it is handed the
  entire thing, and once it has it, it can walk any of the record's own sub-collections and ask
  it anything. After the narrowing, a routine may be told a value, or handed a narrow read-only
  view, but never the record itself. Score for that agent, not a human reader and not a docs
  page.
  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things. First, what
  the record is today - which of the several same-named things in this repo it actually is,
  what a holder can do with one, how it builds the object that answers for it and where it
  pushes itself into that object, and the one layer whose declared job is to make and change
  these records. Second, and this is what decides the answer: every routine downstream that
  ends up holding a whole record, and what it takes out of it once it has one.
  The distinction the whole ticket turns on is possession, and it is decided at the routine's
  own parameter list and at the point it was handed the value - not by anything written on the
  line that uses it. A routine handed a record names it whatever it likes. It can be a declared
  parameter. It can be a local it assigned from a fetch. It can be an entry it reads out of a
  bag of data. It can be a value pulled out of an event by key. It can be an instance the
  routine built itself and never saved. Nothing on the using line ties any of these back to
  what is being held; the tie is at the handover, which is usually in another file and often
  another package.
  And the reverse is what the ticket does NOT ask for: a routine that only ever reaches a
  record through a relation hanging off something it was already holding - a line item, an
  option row, an index row - is not being narrowed, and listing those is the answer a text
  search produces. An answer padded with them has enumerated the easy population and missed
  the one under change.
  Most of the routines under change sit where the record is received rather than where it
  lives: an invoice-item write path handed the record inside a bag of invoice data, an add-on
  listener pulling one out of a view event by key, an importer manufacturing an unsaved
  instance to ask one question of it, a storefront controller walking a record's own related
  set, a variant helper taking one as a parameter, an index refresher, a request object that
  assigned one onto itself, an admin resource constructed with one, and the write repositories
  that take a record and reconcile one of its own sub-collections against submitted data. An answer dense in the
  record's own file and thin everywhere else has enumerated inside one search scope and
  mistaken it for the codebase.
  Every routine must be pinned to the exact file:line with the routine it sits in named, with
  what it takes out of the record said in a phrase, with how the record came into its hands
  accounted for, and with one phrase on what breaks under the narrowing if nothing is done.

steps:
- name: Map the catalogue record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Is the record presented as one connected picture - which of the several same-named
        things in this repo it actually is, what a holder can do with one, and how it produces
        the object that answers for it - rather than as a list of routines or a roll of places
        the name appears? Three things matter most and they are what the next step is built on:
        that the record is passed around WHOLE, so holding one grants the whole surface; that
        the record pushes ITSELF into the object it builds, so that object is itself a holder;
        and that the base class keeps the record in a field, which is where a handed-over
        record ends up living. An answer that locates the line where the object is built, the
        line where the record hands itself over, the base class, the field inside it that
        holds the record, and the failure path when the object cannot be built, has understood
        why the next step is a question about possession at all.
        The step also asks for the declared create-and-change layer at file:line with a phrase
        on what it is responsible for. This is deliberately the easy part - it is the layer
        that hands records out, written down openly - so score coverage and location here, not
        difficulty.
        The step finally asks for a deliberately SMALL third thing: ONE example line where a
        routine is handed a record outright rather than reaching it through something else.
        Score whether that observation is correct and located. Do NOT reward an exhaustive
        enumeration of such lines: it was not asked for, and a long located list is not a
        better answer here than one accurate observation. Could the next step act from this
        orientation with targeted lookups rather than a fresh exploration?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every place the answer names carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine
        or class it lives in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with nothing named
        around it is not specific. This platform gives the record's short name to an empty
        marker interface, to a swap-in proxy class and to unrelated classes in other packages:
        did the answer say WHICH one it settled on and how it told them apart, at file:line
        each, or did it assert one and move on? Is the line that builds the behaviour object
        located, is the line that hands the record over located, is the base class located and
        the field that holds the record named at its own line - or is the mechanism asserted
        from method names and a familiar framework convention?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each part carry one phrase on what it contributes - what holding a whole record
        grants that being told a value does not, what the self-handoff establishes and who
        ends up holding a record because of it, what the create-and-change layer is
        responsible for that the record is not - and does the answer connect those facts to
        why passing the record whole is what the narrowing removes, rather than restating the
        list of routines?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a name
        or a familiar framework convention? This matters more than usual here: closed-book
        this model can already name a third of this record's member surface, so it invites
        confident recitation, and saying which parts were actually opened is worth more here
        than elsewhere. Is anything the session could not resolve named as such rather than
        smoothed over, and is the claim that a routine can be handed a record outright offered
        as something established at a line rather than as a convention it did not check?
- name: Audit every dependent of the catalogue record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        COVERAGE AND THE IN/OUT DECISION, and the two are one question here. Is every routine
        listed separately, pinned to its own line, with what it takes out of the record named?
        A routine named with nothing said about what it draws out of the record is half a row:
        the narrowing replaces the record with a narrow view, and a row that does not say what
        was taken tells the maintainer nothing about which view.
        Score the DISTINCTION the ticket turns on. A routine is in scope when the record is in
        its own hands under a name it chose - a declared parameter, a local assigned from a
        fetch, an entry read out of a bag of data, a value pulled out of an event by key, or an
        instance built in place and never saved. It is OUT of scope when it only ever reaches a
        record through a relation hanging off something else it was already holding. An answer
        that lists every place the record is mentioned, in-scope and out together, is the
        answer a text search produces and has not made the decision the step asked for.
        The record, the object that answers for it, its base class and the create-and-change
        layer are NOT answers here - they were pinned in the previous step, and an answer built
        out of them has answered the easy half.
        Score whether the answer found the routines where the handover is invisible at the
        point of use: a record arriving as an entry in a bag of data, a record pulled out of an
        event by key, a record manufactured in place to be asked one question, a record taken
        as a plain parameter and walked for one of its own sub-collections.
        Is the coverage spread across the areas the routines actually live in - a sales write
        path, an add-on listener, an importer, a storefront controller, option and variant
        helpers, an index refresher, the catalogue write repositories, an admin request object
        - or is it dense in one package and thin outside it?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Three things per row, and all three are required. The routine's exact file:line with
        the routine it sits in named. What it takes out of the record, said concretely - which
        sub-collection it walks, which value it reads - and not as a category. And how the
        record came into its hands: which parameter, which assignment, which key, which
        construction, located where that handover is written, which is often in a different
        file from the routine. A row whose line is right and whose possession is asserted from
        a variable name has not shown its answer. A bare filename is not specific; a group of
        routines offered together under one shared reason with no location on any of them is
        not an answer at all. Where several routines live in one file, are they listed
        separately with their own lines?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each row carry one phrase on what depends on holding the whole record - which
        behaviour, which decision, what a narrow view would have to expose for this routine to
        keep working - rather than restating that the routine uses the record? The rows where
        the routine walks a sub-collection deserve the sharpest justification: those are the
        ones a narrow view cannot serve without deciding what to expose. Does the answer say
        so?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Where the answer could not establish whether a routine holds a whole record or only
        reaches one through something else, does it say so and say what it would need to open,
        rather than counting it in by default? Is the coverage claim honest - if the session
        ran short, does it name what it had not looked at yet, or does it close on what it has
        and imply completeness? Confident inclusion of a routine on the strength of a variable
        name, with no handover established, is the specific overreach this criterion exists to
        catch.
